<%= l_ui_title_bar(
  title: "Devise",
  breadcrumbs: [
    ["Home", root_path],
    ["Integrations", integrations_path]
  ]
) %>

<p class="mt-4">Devise is fully supported but entirely optional. The engine works without any authentication system.</p>

<h2 class="mt-8 l-ui-heading--section">When Devise is installed</h2>

<p class="mt-4">The engine automatically detects Devise and provides:</p>

<ul class="l-ui-list mt-4">
  <li>Styled sign in, registration, account settings, and password reset, confirmation, and unlock views</li>
  <li>Header links that adapt to authentication state</li>
  <li>User info in the sidebar when signed in, with a menu holding Settings and Logout</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="mt-8 l-ui-heading--section">Installation</h2>

<p class="mt-4">Add Devise to your application:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>bundle add devise</code></pre>

<p class="mt-4">Run the Devise installer and generate a User model:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>bin/rails generate devise:install</code></pre>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>bin/rails generate devise User</code></pre>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>bin/rails db:migrate</code></pre>

<p class="mt-4">Add Devise routes to your <code>routes.rb</code>:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>devise_for :users</code></pre>

<h2 class="mt-8 l-ui-heading--section">Configuration</h2>

<p class="mt-4">The engine uses <code>current_user</code> by default. If your app uses a different method, configure it in an initializer:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>Layered::Ui.current_user_method = :current_admin</code></pre>

<p class="mt-4">If your Devise mapping isn't <code>:user</code> (e.g. <code>devise_for :admins</code>), also set the scope so the header and sidebar link to the right registration, session, and logout paths:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>Layered::Ui.devise_scope = :admin</code></pre>

<p class="mt-4">The sidebar displays the signed-in user's email. If your user model has a <code>name</code> attribute, it will be displayed as well. To support this, add a name column to your users migration:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>add_column :users, :name, :string</code></pre>

<p class="mt-4">Then permit the name parameter in your <code>ApplicationController</code>:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>before_action :configure_permitted_parameters, if: :devise_controller?

protected

def configure_permitted_parameters
  devise_parameter_sanitizer.permit(:sign_up, keys: [:name])
end</code></pre>

<h2 class="mt-8 l-ui-heading--section">Settings screen</h2>

<p class="mt-4">Devise's <code>registrations#edit</code> action is styled as a Settings screen at <code>/users/edit</code>, with the account details under an "Account" tab. It lets a signed-in user update their email (and name, if the model has one), change their password, and cancel their account. All changes require the current password, as Devise expects.</p>

<p class="mt-4">When a user is signed in, the sidebar shows a menu button next to their name and email holding Settings and Logout, so no wiring is needed. To link to the screen elsewhere, use the <code>l_ui_edit_registration_path</code> helper, which respects the configured Devise scope and returns <code>nil</code> when the route is not available:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>&lt;%= l_ui_navigation_item "Settings", l_ui_edit_registration_path %&gt;</code></pre>

<p class="mt-4">If your layout hides application chrome on Devise views, make an exception for this screen with <code>l_ui_settings_screen?</code> - it is part of the signed-in app, so it should keep the navigation:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>&lt;% unless devise_controller? &amp;&amp; !l_ui_settings_screen? %&gt;
  &lt;% content_for :l_ui_navigation_items do %&gt;
    ...
  &lt;% end %&gt;
&lt;% end %&gt;</code></pre>

<p class="mt-4">If your user model has a <code>name</code> attribute, permit it for account updates as well as sign up:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>def configure_permitted_parameters
  devise_parameter_sanitizer.permit(:sign_up, keys: [:name])
  devise_parameter_sanitizer.permit(:account_update, keys: [:name])
end</code></pre>

<h2 class="mt-8 l-ui-heading--section">Ejecting a view</h2>

<p class="mt-4">Every view the engine provides can be replaced by copying it to the same path in your application. Application views resolve before engine views, so <code>app/views/devise/registrations/edit.html.erb</code> takes over the Settings screen and nothing else changes.</p>

<p class="mt-4">Locate the original to copy:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>bundle info layered-ui-rails --path</code></pre>

<p class="mt-4">An ejected view lives in your application rather than the engine, so it may use plain Tailwind utilities alongside the <code>l-ui-*</code> classes - your Tailwind build scans it.</p>

<p class="mt-4">One caveat: <code>bin/rails generate devise:views</code> copies Devise's own unstyled views into <code>app/views/devise/</code>, which replaces <em>every</em> styled view provided here, not just the one you meant to change. Eject the individual views you need instead.</p>

<h2 class="mt-8 l-ui-heading--section">Helper methods</h2>

<p class="mt-4">These helpers allow your views to work with or without Devise:</p>

<div class="l-ui-table-container mt-4">
  <table class="l-ui-table">
    <caption class="l-ui-sr-only">Devise helpers</caption>
    <thead class="l-ui-table__header">
      <tr>
        <th class="l-ui-table__header-cell" scope="col">Helper</th>
        <th class="l-ui-table__header-cell" scope="col">Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="l-ui-table__body">
      <tr>
        <th class="l-ui-table__cell l-ui-table__cell--primary" scope="row"><code>l_ui_devise_installed?</code></th>
        <td class="l-ui-table__cell">Returns true if Devise is available</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th class="l-ui-table__cell l-ui-table__cell--primary" scope="row"><code>l_ui_user_signed_in?</code></th>
        <td class="l-ui-table__cell">Safe wrapper for <code>user_signed_in?</code> that returns false when Devise is not installed</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
